''garent'', a warrant, also a warranter, supporter, defender, protector, = Pr. ''garen'', ''guaren'' = Sp. Pg. ''garente'' = OIt. ''guarento'' (ML. reflex ''warantum'', ''warrantum'', ''waranda''), a warrant; perhaps orig. a ppr. of OF. ''warir'', ''warer'', defend, keep, < OHG. ''warjan'', ''werjan'', MHG. ''wern'', ''weren'', G. ''wehren'', protect: see ''ware''<sup>1</sup>, ''wear''<sup>2</sup>. Hence ''warrantise'', ''warranty'', ''guaranty'', etc. Cf. ''warren''.] '''1†.''' Protector; protection; defense; safeguard.
{{smaller block|He griped his suerde in bothe hondes, and whom that he raught a full stroke was so harde smyten that noon armure was his ''warante'' fro deth.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 408.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Thy safe ''warrand'' we will be.</poem>
{{right|''Hobie Noble'' (Child’s Ballads, VI. 100).}}}}
'''2.''' Security; guaranty; assurance; voucher; attestation; evidence; pledge; that which attests or proves.
{{smaller block|His promise is our plain ''warrant'' that in his name what we ask we shall receive.
{{right|''St. Cyprian'', in Hooker’s Eccles. Polity, v. 35.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Before Emilia here
I give thee ''warrant'' of thy place.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, iii. 3. 20.}}}}
{{smaller block|Any bill, ''warrant'', quittance, or obligation.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., i. 1. 10.}}}}
{{smaller block|His books are by themselves the ''warrant'' of the fame which he so widely gained.
{{right|''Stubbs'', Medieval and Modern Hist., p. 376.}}}}

